 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE COMEDY CARPET. Laid at the foot of the Tower in 2011, it is a vast floor of granite and concrete set with the jokes, songs and catchphrases of more than a thousand comedians and writers who played Blackpool. You read it with your feet. People stop dead in the middle of it, laughing at the floor.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_comedy_carpet",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Comedy Carpet"
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "BLACKPOOL TOWER. Inspired by the Eiffel Tower in Paris, it opened on 14 May 1894 and stands 158 metres high. Between its four legs is the Tower Circus, which has run every year since the tower opened; at the grand finale the ring sinks and floods with water. Up top, a glass floor lets you look straight down at the prom. Brave people only.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_tower",
   "title": "Blackpool Tower"
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "THE ILLUMINATIONS. In 1879 eight arc lamps lit the promenade, and folk called it 'artificial sunshine'. The lights as we know them began in 1912, when Princess Louise opened the new Princess Parade. Today they run for about six miles, from Starr Gate to Bispham, every autumn. The switch-on is a big night: a famous face throws the switch and the whole prom goes 'ooh'.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_illuminations",
   "title": "The Illuminations"
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "THREE PIERS. Blackpool is the only town in Britain with three: North Pier (1863), Central Pier (1868) and South Pier (1893). North was the genteel one, with concerts and a band. Central was where the working folk danced. You could tell the town's whole social order by which pier you walked along.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_three_piers",
   "title": "Three Piers"
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "THE TRAMS. Blackpool's tramway opened in 1885, one of the first electric street tramways anywhere, and the oldest still running. The double-deck 'Balloon' trams date from the 1930s. For years, Blackpool kept its trams when almost every other British town scrapped theirs.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_trams",
   "title": "The Trams"
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 47,
   "text": "THE DONKEYS. There have been donkey rides on Blackpool sands since Victorian times. The donkeys have long had their own rules: a day off each week, a weight limit for riders, and a proper lunch break. Better terms than most of the people leading them, the donkey men say.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_donkeys",
   "title": "The Beach Donkeys"
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "WAKES WEEKS. In the mill towns of Lancashire and Yorkshire each town shut its mills for its own holiday week. Whole towns, Bolton or Oldham or Burnley, would arrive in Blackpool on the same trains, stay in the same guest houses and go home together, pockets empty, sunburnt, happy.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_wakes_weeks",
   "title": "Wakes Weeks"
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "TRAM STOP: North Pier, the Tower, Pleasure Beach, Starr Gate. Next tram: when it gets here. Fares, please."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 53,
   "text": "THE BIG ONE. When it opened at the Pleasure Beach in 1994 it was the tallest rollercoaster in the world: a lift hill of 65 metres and a first drop at about 74 miles an hour. The Pleasure Beach itself dates from 1896 and still runs wooden coasters from the 1920s and 30s. The queue for the front seat is a Blackpool tradition all of its own.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_big_one",
   "title": "The Big One"
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 50,
   "text": "SIR STANLEY MATTHEWS. The wizard of the dribble played for Blackpool from 1947 to 1961. In the 1953 FA Cup Final, the 'Matthews Final', Blackpool were 3-1 down to Bolton and won 4-3. He was the first footballer to be knighted, and he played top-flight football until he was fifty.",
   "plaque": "lancashire_matthews",
   "title": "Stanley Matthews"
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 63,
   "text": "SOUTH: Lytham St Annes and the coast road to Southport. Mind the donkey crossing."
  },
  {
   "x": 72,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "lancashire_crown_green_bowls",
   "title": "Crown Green Bowls",
   "text": "CROWN GREEN BOWLS. A crown green is not flat: it rises to a hump in the middle, the crown, and the woods are lopsided, so they curve in as they slow. The jack can be rolled in any direction, so every end is a different puzzle. It is a game of the North and the Midlands, and the Waterloo Hotel in Blackpool hosts the Waterloo Handicap, one of its best-known tournaments, every summer."
  }
 ],
